Experiment

a) Write a CPU-bound C program and an I/O-bound C program, and observe the effect of their CPU share using the top command and its variants.
b) Observe and understand the process map of the previous two programs using the pmap command.

Software Required

1. Operating System: Linux / Ubuntu
2. Compiler: GCC
3. Text Editor: Nano / Vim
4. Commands: top, ps, pmap, pgrep, gcc

Theory

• CPU-Bound Process: A CPU-bound process spends most of its execution time performing calculations and requires substantial CPU processing time.
• I/O-Bound Process: An I/O-bound process spends much of its time performing input/output operations or waiting for them to complete. It generally consumes less CPU time than a CPU-bound process.
• Top Command: The top command is used to monitor running processes and system resource utilization. It displays real-time process information such as PID, CPU usage, memory usage, etc.
• Pmap Command: The pmap command displays the virtual memory map or memory footprint of a specific process.
• Process Memory Map: A process memory map contains virtual memory regions such as program code, data, stack, heap, etc.

Procedure


A) Writing and Running the Programs

1. Open the Linux terminal.
2. Create the CPU-bound program and save it.
3. Compile the program using the gcc compiler.
4. Execute the compiled CPU-bound program.
5. Create the I/O-bound program and save it.
6. Compile the I/O-bound program using the gcc compiler.
7. Execute the compiled I/O-bound program.
8. Keep both programs running in the background or in separate terminal sessions.

B) Observe CPU Utilization

9. Open another terminal window.
10. Execute the top command.
11. Identify the CPU-bound and I/O-bound processes using their process IDs (PIDs).
12. Observe the %CPU, %MEM, and process state (STATUS) of both processes.
13. Press P inside the top interface to sort running processes by CPU utilization.
14. Use the ps command to compare the CPU usage metrics of both programs.
15. Record your observations.

C) Observe Process Memory Map Using pmap

16. Find the PID of the CPU-bound process.
17. Execute the pmap command followed by the PID for the CPU-bound process.
18. Observe the displayed virtual memory mappings.
19. Find the PID of the I/O-bound process
20. Execute the pmap command for the I/O-bound process.
21. Observe the virtual memory mapping.
22. Use pmap -x to examine extended memory statistics such as mapping size and PSS (Proportional Set Size).
23. Compare the memory maps of both processes and record the observations.
24. Stop both programs using the Ctrl + C shortcut.

Commands Used

• gcc cpu.c -o cpu
• ./cpu
• gcc io.c -o io
• ./io
• top
• ps -C cpu,io -o pid,comm,%cpu,%mem,stat
• pgrep -a -x cpu
• pgrep -a -x io
• pmap PID
• pmap -x PID

Result

The CPU-bound and I/O-bound C programs were successfully compiled and executed. Their CPU utilization was observed and compared using the top command and its variants. The process memory maps were examined using the pmap command, and their extended memory statistics were studied using pmap -x.
